From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Birk Skyum <birk.skyum@pm.me>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 11:22:25 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] reset: gpio: keep the reset-gpio device under a parent that
 stays

reset-gpio auxiliary devices are never removed, but they are created as
children of the GPIO *device* (gpiochipN), which is unregistered whenever
the GPIO controller's driver unbinds and is created anew when it binds
again. On the Lenovo Yoga Slim 7x the WSA884x speakers share reset GPIOs on
the LPASS LPI pin controller. That controller depends on clocks the audio
DSP provides, so it unbinds and rebinds whenever the ADSP restarts (a DSP
crash recovery, a driver reload, or booting the DSPs again after
hibernation). The reset-gpio devices are then left under a deleted parent,
their next probe fails with "really_probe: driver_sysfs_add failed", the
speakers never get their reset line and the sound card stays gone.

Create the reset-gpio device under the device that provides the GPIO
controller, which stays, and add a managed device link to it so the
reset-gpio driver unbinds with the controller's driver and probes again
when it is back.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/reset/core.c | 27 +++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/reset/core.c b/drivers/reset/core.c
index 38e189d04..fad597805 100644
--- a/drivers/reset/core.c
+++ b/drivers/reset/core.c
@@ -1070,9 +1070,21 @@ static int __reset_add_reset_gpio_device(struct fwnode_handle *fwnode,
 	}
 
 	lflags = GPIO_PERSISTENT | (flags & GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW);
-	parent = gpio_device_to_device(gdev);
 	properties[prop++] = PROPERTY_ENTRY_STRING("compatible", "reset-gpio");
-	properties[prop++] = PROPERTY_ENTRY_GPIO("reset-gpios", parent->fwnode, offset, lflags);
+	properties[prop++] = PROPERTY_ENTRY_GPIO("reset-gpios",
+						 gpio_device_to_device(gdev)->fwnode,
+						 offset, lflags);
+
+	/*
+	 * The reset-gpio device is never removed, but the GPIO device goes
+	 * away whenever the GPIO controller's driver unbinds, for example the
+	 * LPASS pin controller when the audio DSP restarts. A reset-gpio
+	 * device left under the old GPIO device can never bind again
+	 * ("driver_sysfs_add failed"). Put it under the device that provides
+	 * the GPIO controller instead, which stays, and link it to that
+	 * device below so it follows the controller's driver.
+	 */
+	parent = gpio_device_to_device(gdev)->parent ?: gpio_device_to_device(gdev);
 
 	/* Not freed on success, because it is persisent subsystem data. */
 	rgpio_dev = kzalloc_obj(*rgpio_dev);
@@ -1097,6 +1109,17 @@ static int __reset_add_reset_gpio_device(struct fwnode_handle *fwnode,
 	if (ret)
 		goto err_del_swnode;
 
+	/*
+	 * Unbind when the GPIO controller's driver unbinds and probe again
+	 * when it is back, so the reset controller never holds GPIO
+	 * descriptors of a removed chip.
+	 */
+	if (parent != gpio_device_to_device(gdev) &&
+	    !device_link_add(&rgpio_dev->adev.dev, parent,
+			     DL_FLAG_AUTOPROBE_CONSUMER))
+		pr_warn("Failed to link reset-gpio device %s to its GPIO controller\n",
+			dev_name(&rgpio_dev->adev.dev));
+
 	reset_gpio_add_devlink(fwnode, rgpio_dev);
 	list_add(&rgpio_dev->list, &reset_gpio_lookup_list);
 
-- 
2.56.1

